# Line endings.
#
# The package is developed on Windows and run on all three systems, so
# most files are left to git's own normalization.  The shell scripts are
# not: a script checked out with CRLF fails on Linux with
#
#     /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# which says nothing about the real cause.  They are pinned to LF, on
# every platform, whatever core.autocrlf is set to locally.

*.sh text eol=lf
CODE/unix/* text eol=lf

# The wrappers in CODE/unix/ carry no extension -- they are typed as
# `rcg`, `rcn2`, `add_eav` and so on -- so the directory rule above is
# what covers them, and it has to stay ahead of any rule that would
# treat an extensionless file as binary.

# Windows batch files are the mirror image: cmd.exe is content with LF
# for the most part, but a label or a goto at the end of a line without
# CR has been known to misbehave, so these keep CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
